{
  "abstract": "Ordinary graph covers can disperse dense configurations, suggesting a possible construction of a ten-chromatic biplanar graph by lifting a nine-chromatic source and adding a universal vertex. We prove coloring obstructions for two such sources. First, every biplanar cone over an ordinary finite cover of $K_6\\vee C_{2s+1}$ is nine-colorable, with no sheet or coherence restriction. A short Brooks reduction reserves independent rim fibers and colors the remaining clique subcover first. Second, every connected nontrivial regular cover of $M=C_5[K_3,K_4,K_3,K_3,K_4]$ is eight-colorable. Explicit source partitions reduce nonidentity triangle monodromy to a cubic coloring repair; otherwise the cover is a cyclic clique blow-up with two concatenable palette patterns. Third, every three-sheet cover of $M$ whose individual bags lift to disjoint cliques is eight-colorable or contains a trivial source component. A minimum-break argument and a replacement of overlapping local defects reduce this irregular family to 1,100 positive coloring certificates, independently verified with complete orbit coverage. We also certify a nine-chromatic union of alternative matchings, showing why a stronger universal-coloring policy fails, and give a palette extension for one internally nontrivial triangle. The union obstruction is not an ordinary-cover lower bound. The results close the stated cone constructions and identify internal incoherence as a remaining triple-cover boundary; arbitrary irregular covers and the Earth–Moon problem remain open.",
